{
  "lessons": [
    {
      "lesson_title": "Understand Neural Networks",
      "slides": [
        {
          "slide_title": "Neural Network Structure",
          "bullets": [
            {
              "text": "A system of connected nodes arranged in layers that process data."
            },
            {
              "text": "Data goes in one end, flows through layers, and a result comes out."
            },
            {
              "text": "Training adjusts internal weights each time the model gets something wrong."
            },
            {
              "text": "After enough training it handles examples it has never seen before."
            },
            {
              "text": "All modern AI — LLMs, image generators, voice models — are neural networks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "slide_title": "Model Types Overview",
          "bullets": [
            {
              "text": "Frontier models are the most powerful proprietary models (GPT-4o, Claude, Gemini)."
            },
            {
              "text": "Accessed via API and charged per use; weights are kept private by the company."
            },
            {
              "text": "Open source models have publicly released weights (Llama, Mistral, Qwen)."
            },
            {
              "text": "The models used by the PhillipCapital chgatbot is open source."
            },
            {
              "text": "Open source gives full control and can be cheaper at scale but needs own infrastructure."
            },
            {
              "text": "Choice depends on capability needed, control required, and budget."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "slide_title": "Embedding Model Function",
          "bullets": [
            {
              "text": "Converts text (or images, audio) into a list of numbers called a vector."
            },
            {
              "text": "Similar meanings produce vectors that are close together in numerical space."
            },
            {
              "text": "\"Dog\" and \"puppy\" will be close; \"dog\" and \"skyscraper\" will be far apart."
            },
            {
              "text": "Turns unstructured language into something computers can compare mathematically."
            },
            {
              "text": "Foundation of search, recommendations, and any \"find similar content\" feature."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "slide_title": "Cosine Similarity Explained",
          "bullets": [
            {
              "text": "Measures how similar two vectors are by calculating the angle between them."
            },
            {
              "text": "Score of 1.0 = identical meaning; -1.0 = opposite; 0 = unrelated."
            },
            {
              "text": "Uses angle not straight-line distance — unaffected by length of text."
            },
            {
              "text": "Used in vector search to find the most relevant content for a query."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "slide_title": "Vector Database Overview",
          "bullets": [
            {
              "text": "A database built to store embeddings and search through them at speed."
            },
            {
              "text": "Returns the closest matching vectors to a query — called nearest neighbour search."
            },
            {
              "text": "Unlike SQL, finds fuzzy meaning-based matches rather than exact keyword matches."
            },
            {
              "text": "Popular options: Pinecone, Weaviate, Chroma, pgvector."
            },
            {
              "text": "The backbone of any RAG system connecting AI to your private documents."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{


  "lessons": [
    {
      "slides": [
        {
          "script": "We'll start by examining why AI is crucial for career growth at Phillip Capital. The financial sector is evolving rapidly, making AI fluency not just a competitive advantage, but a necessity for advancement. AI handles routine tasks like data entry, account reconciliation, and financial reporting in a fraction of the time. It processes vast amounts of unstructured data—such as global economic indicators and social media sentiment—to generate more accurate financial plans. Real-time anomaly detection helps mitigate risks before they escalate. By offloading data-heavy tasks, you can focus on strategic advisory roles, guiding high-level corporate decisions. Remember, while AI won't replace your job, those leveraging its capabilities will outpace those who don't."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{


  "lessons": [
    {
      "slides": [
        {
          "script": "We'll start by exploring the LLM tools available at Phillip Capital. Your goal is to leverage these models to enhance your work. You'll gain access to our internal chatbot application, a powerful LLM designed specifically for our organization. This tool can answer work-related queries, search the web, and even analyze documents you upload. By utilizing these capabilities, you can streamline tasks and access information more efficiently."
        },
        {
          "script": "Next, let's examine the advanced features of multimodal LLMs. These models can process and generate various data types, including text, images, audio, and video. For instance, the LMS system you use relies on multimodal LLMs to create comprehensive training materials that combine visual and textual content. This versatility allows you to engage with information in multiple formats, enhancing your learning and productivity."
        },
        {
          "script": "Finally, we'll discuss AI agents and their tools. AI agents act as orchestrators, enabling the LLM to access various functions. These tools range from simple tasks like checking the weather to complex actions such as scheduling meetings directly with your Google Calendar. By integrating these tools, you can automate routine tasks and focus on higher-value work, improving your overall efficiency."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{


  "lessons": [
    {
      "slides": [
        {
          "script": "We'll start by understanding neural networks, the foundation of modern AI. A neural network is a system of connected nodes arranged in layers that process data. Data enters one end, flows through these layers, and produces a result. During training, the model adjusts internal weights each time it makes a mistake. After sufficient training, it can handle examples it has never seen before. All modern AI, including LLMs, image generators, and voice models, relies on neural networks as its core engine."
        },
        {
          "script": "Next, let's explore the different types of AI models. Frontier models, like GPT-4o, Claude, and Gemini, are the most powerful proprietary models. They're accessed via API, charged per use, and their weights remain private. Open source models, such as Llama, Mistral, and Qwen, have publicly released weights. The PhillipCapital chatbot uses an open source model, offering full control and potential cost savings at scale, though it requires your own infrastructure. Your choice depends on the required capability, control, and budget."
        },
        {
          "script": "Now, let's examine embedding models. These models convert text, images, or audio into numerical vectors. Similar meanings result in vectors that are close together in numerical space, while dissimilar ones are far apart. For example, 'dog' and 'puppy' would be close, but 'dog' and 'skyscraper' would be far. This process transforms unstructured language into a format computers can compare mathematically, forming the basis for search, recommendations, and finding similar content."
        },
        {
          "script": "Let's now look at cosine similarity. This measures how similar two vectors are by calculating the angle between them. A score of 1.0 indicates identical meaning, -1.0 means opposite, and 0 signifies unrelated. Unlike straight-line distance, it uses the angle, making it unaffected by text length. Cosine similarity is crucial in vector search, helping find the most relevant content for any query."
        },
        {
          "script": "Finally, we'll cover vector databases. These databases store embeddings and enable fast searches by returning the closest matching vectors—known as nearest neighbour search. Unlike SQL, which finds exact keyword matches, vector databases identify fuzzy meaning-based matches. Popular options include Pinecone, Weaviate, Chroma, and pgvector. They serve as the backbone of RAG systems, connecting AI to your private documents."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

